Popular messaging app WhatsApp <a href="https://blog.whatsapp.com/say-it-at-the-right-time-with-scheduled-messages">was today updated</a> with an option to schedule messages, which the company says was one of its most requested features.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/whatsapp-scheduled-messages.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1079563" /><br/>
WhatsApp users can create a message and schedule it to arrive from 10 minutes to 14 days later in one-to-one chats and group chats. Scheduling a message can be done by pressing and holding the send button after composing and then choosing a date and time. Scheduled messages work with text, photos, and videos, and will still send even if a user's device is offline.<br/>
<br/>
There is an option to see all scheduled messages and delete them before they're sent if needed. Scheduled messages are end-to-end encrypted and look like any other WhatsApp message, with no indication on the receiver's end that the message was scheduled.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's iMessage service already has a Send Later feature that supports scheduling messages, so WhatsApp is catching up with Apple's built-in messaging option.<br/>
<br/>
